<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <meta charset="utf-8" />
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
        <title>About — Cragcast</title>
        <meta name="description" content="Learn how Cragcast rates climbing conditions using weather and air quality forecasts from Open-Meteo and CAMS." />
        <style>
            :root {
                --page: #ffffff;
                --text: #14161a;
                --muted: #62686f;
                --hair: rgba(20, 22, 26, 0.1);
                --go-fg: #2c6b2e;
            }
            @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
                :root {
                    --page: #0e1014;
                    --text: #e7e9ec;
                    --muted: #969ca4;
                    --hair: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
                    --go-fg: #93d79c;
                }
            }

            * {
                box-sizing: border-box;
            }
            html {
                -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%;
            }
            body {
                margin: 0;
                background: var(--page);
                color: var(--text);
                font:
                    15px/1.6 system-ui,
                    -apple-system,
                    "Segoe UI",
                    Roboto,
                    sans-serif;
                -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
            }
            .app {
                max-width: 540px;
                margin: 0 auto;
                padding: 22px 16px 48px;
            }

            a {
                color: var(--go-fg);
                text-decoration: none;
            }
            a:hover {
                text-decoration: underline;
            }

            .back {
                display: inline-block;
                margin-bottom: 16px;
                font-size: 13px;
                color: var(--muted);
            }

            header {
                margin-bottom: 22px;
            }
            .wordmark {
                font-size: 21px;
                font-weight: 650;
                letter-spacing: -0.02em;
            }
            .tagline {
                color: var(--muted);
                font-size: 13px;
                margin-top: 2px;
            }

            h2 {
                font-size: 20px;
                font-weight: 700;
                margin: 20px 0 8px;
            }
            p {
                margin: 0 0 14px;
            }

            :focus-visible {
                outline: 2px solid var(--text);
                outline-offset: 2px;
                border-radius: 4px;
            }
        </style>
    </head>
    <body>
        <div class="app">
            <a class="back" href="/">&larr; Back to forecast</a>

            <header>
                <div class="wordmark">Cragcast</div>
                <div class="tagline">
                    Climbing conditions within driving distance of Seattle
                </div>
            </header>

            <main>
                <p>
                    Cragcast checks the weather forecast for crags near Seattle and
                    rates climbing conditions with traffic light colors: green for
                    good, yellow for marginal, and red for poor conditions.
                </p>

                <h2>How we rate conditions</h2>
                <p>
                    We rate temperature, sun exposure, humidity (using dew point), wind,
                    and available air quality together for each hour. Morning covers
                    7am–1pm and afternoon covers 1pm–7pm, in local time.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A window is good with at least four consecutive good hours and no
                    poor hours. It is poor with at least four poor hours, consecutive
                    or otherwise. Other combinations are marginal.
                </p>
                <p>
                    We use seven days of hourly rain history to estimate surface wetness
                    and, at crags known to seep, seepage potential. Each rain hour adds
                    moisture; temperature, dew point, wind, sunlight, aspect, and rock
                    type affect surface drying. Seepage fades more slowly. The estimates
                    start dry seven days back and continue through the seven-day forecast.
                    These are uncalibrated estimates, not observations or precise drying
                    times. Humidity alone does not mean wet rock. The displayed 24h and
                    72h rain totals are context, not color thresholds. Forecast rain can
                    worsen the whole window's rating. The worse half sets the day's color.
                </p>
                <p>
                    An area is green when most crags are good, red when most are poor
                    and none are good, and yellow otherwise.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The rating system is still being refined. We'll keep adjusting it
                    to better reflect conditions at the crag.
                </p>

                <h2>How to support</h2>
                <p>
                    Cragcast is free. To support it, please take care of the crags
                    it covers: pack out any trash you find, don't top-rope
                    directly on fixed gear, and support local climbing
                    organizations. Weather forecasts come from
                    <a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo</a>, which also
                    provides air quality forecasts using data from the
                    <a href="https://atmosphere.copernicus.eu/">Copernicus Atmosphere
                    Monitoring Service (CAMS)</a>. Please support them too.
                </p>
                <h2>Get in touch</h2>
                <p>
                    Questions, corrections, or crag suggestions? Email
                    <a href="mailto:anton.evseev@proton.me">anton.evseev@proton.me</a>.
                </p>

                <h2>Data license</h2>
                <p>
                    Open-Meteo forecast data is licensed under
                    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>.
                    Cragcast uses this data to calculate climbing condition ratings.
                </p>
            </main>

            {{template "footer" .Footer}}
        </div>
    </body>
</html>
